  "articlePanels": [
    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>A care home is only as safe as its least-trained shift. A training and supervision matrix is the map of who is current on what, moving and handling, safeguarding, fire, medication, and who is overdue. Its whole value is timing: it should make a lapse visible before it happens, while there is time to book the course or schedule the supervision. The real question is not what to track, every home knows the mandatory list, but how to keep the matrix current so it is a live map at inspection, not a spreadsheet that stopped being true in spring.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>A training and supervision matrix tracks which staff are current on mandatory training and whose supervisions are on schedule.</li><li>Inspectors look at whether staff are suitably trained, supported and supervised, under CQC's well-led and staffing expectations in England and the equivalents across the UK.</li><li>The real risk hides in bank and agency staff, who often fall outside the core matrix; a check that misses them misses the gap.</li><li>There is no single national mandatory training list set by statute for every role; work from your policies, the relevant guidance and any funded frameworks.</li><li>The matrix does not train anyone; keeping it current and acting on the gaps does.</li><li>Quickler runs the matrix check as a recurring audit and records the finding. It is not an HR or learning management system.</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Currency","heading":"Mandatory training, kept in date","html":"<p>Every care home carries a set of training that staff must hold and refresh: moving and handling, safeguarding adults, fire safety, infection prevention and control, basic life support or first aid, medication where relevant, and more depending on the service and the roles. The exact list and the refresh intervals come from your own policies, the relevant sector guidance and any funded frameworks, rather than one national statute, so build the matrix around what applies to your home.</p><p>The point of tracking currency is to see the lapse coming. A refresher that expires next month should show up now, as an amber flag, so it is booked before it lapses, not discovered red at inspection. A recurring matrix check does exactly this: it surfaces what is due and what has slipped, and turns each into an action. Always work from your current policies and guidance, and treat this page as general information, not legal advice.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Supervision","heading":"One-to-ones and appraisal on schedule","html":"<p>Training currency is only half the matrix. Supervision, the regular one-to-one between a staff member and their line manager, plus annual appraisal, is the other half, and inspectors ask about it directly. Good supervision is where competence is checked, concerns are raised, wellbeing is supported and development is planned. A home that trains well but supervises poorly still has a gap, because training on a certificate is not the same as competence on the floor.</p><p>The matrix should track when each person was last supervised and when the next is due, on the schedule your policy sets, and it should cover everyone: permanent, bank and agency. Supervisions slip quietly, a busy month, a manager off, and a quarter goes by. A recurring check catches the slip while it is one missed session, not a pattern. Quickler records which supervisions are current and which are overdue, so the manager sees the schedule holding or slipping at a glance.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Scope, honestly","heading":"The check, not the HR system","html":"<p>Quickler runs the matrix check and records the finding: this refresher is in date, this one lapses next month, this supervision is overdue. It turns the gaps into actions with owners and dates, and gives a group a live view of training and supervision currency across every home, so a regional lead sees which homes are on top of it and which are drifting.</p><p>It is not a learning management system and it is not an HR system. It does not deliver training, host courses, or hold personnel files and contracts. Those live in your HR and learning platforms. Quickler audits whether the matrix is current and the gaps are being closed, and captures that as evidence, without becoming the system of record for your staff data. It is the audit and evidence layer around your HR and training systems, not a replacement for them.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>What is a training and supervision matrix?</h3><p>It is the map of which staff are current on which mandatory training, and whose supervisions and appraisals are on schedule. It exists to make lapses visible in time to close them, a refresher due next month or a supervision that has slipped, across the whole team including bank and agency staff.</p><h3>What training is mandatory for care home staff?</h3><p>There is no single national statutory list covering every role. Typical mandatory training includes moving and handling, safeguarding, fire safety, infection control and basic life support, with more depending on the service, but the exact list and refresh intervals come from your policies, sector guidance and any funded frameworks. Build the matrix around what applies to your home and check the current guidance.</p><h3>Does Quickler replace our HR or training platform?</h3><p>No. Quickler runs the matrix check as a recurring audit and records the finding, what is current, what has lapsed, what supervision is overdue. It does not deliver training or hold personnel files; those stay in your learning and HR systems. Quickler is the audit and evidence layer that checks the matrix is current and the gaps are being closed.</p><h3>Can I run the matrix check over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The manager works through the check in their existing WhatsApp chat, with text or a voice note, recording what is in date and what has lapsed, and the report and action list generate automatically. No separate app or login is required.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/cqc-compliance-audit-checklist-uk.html\">CQC compliance audit checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-compliance-checklist-monthly-uk.html\">Monthly care home compliance checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/safeguarding-audit-care-home-uk.html\">Safeguarding audit for care homes</a></li></ul>"}
